==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|faquir||obsolete}}
* {{alter|en|faqir|faquir}}
* {{alter|en|faqueer||dated}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|ar|فَقِير|t=poor man}}.
{{root|en|ar|ف ق ر}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/fəˈkiɹ/|/fəˈkɪəɹ/|/fɑˈkiɹ/|/ˈfeɪkəɹ/}}
* {{homophones|en|faker}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪə(ɹ)|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|Islam}} A [[faqir]], owning no personal property and usually living solely off [[alms]].
# {{lb|en|Hinduism|more|_|loosely}} An [[ascetic]] [[mendicant]], especially one who [[perform]]s [[feat]]s of [[endurance]] or apparent [[magic]].
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=16|passage=The preposterous altruism too!{{...}}Resist not evil. It is an insane immolation of self—as bad intrinsically as '''fakirs''' stabbing themselves or anchorites warping their spines in caves scarcely large enough for a fair-sized dog.}}
# {{lb|en|derogatory}} Someone who takes advantage of the [[gullible]] through [[fakery]], especially of a spiritual or religious nature.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1905|title=Eclectic Magazine, Foreign Literature, Science, and Art
|passage=He denounces no one until he has all the damaging facts in hand, very frequently backed up with affidavits. He 'Lawsonized' certain stock jobbers and financial '''fakirs''' of London before the Boston advertising man was heard of.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1927|title=The Rotarian|page=30
|passage=&quot;But a stranger who had come up to the group just at this point, when they were pronouncing the soup delicious, laughed aloud. &quot;&#39;What a set of fools you all are!&#39; he cried. &#39;This tramp is just a '''fakir'''. That stone had nothing to do with the soup."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1994|author=Michael Barry Miller|title=Shanghai on the Métro: Spies, Intrigue, and the French Between the Wars|publisher=Univ of California Press|isbn=9780520085190|page=252
|passage=He was, as the undercover agent concluded, a fabulous raconteur or, as one other person summed him up, &quot;a monumental '''fakir''' and liar.&quot;}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Gelett Burgess|title=The Heart Line: A Drama of San Francisco|publisher=Lulu.com|isbn=9781605433851|page=175
|passage=From what I hear of him he&#39;s a '''fakir''', and I won&#39;t encourage him in his attempts to get into society at my expense.}}

